# Every path whose bytes are hashed is pinned to the bytes git stores. On a
# machine with `core.autocrlf=true` a fresh checkout would otherwise write these
# files back as CRLF and the recorded `content_sha256` / `sha256:` anchors would
# stop recomputing. The gate goes red rather than passing silently - but a
# registry that only verifies on one machine is not a registry.
#
# Four roots, no gaps. `artifacts/*.json` was one line away from missing
# `artifacts/report/`, and the secret gate recomputes `sha256:` anchors on two
# files under `research/` as well. A new tree that gets hashed adds its root
# here; the list is closed, not illustrative.

artifacts/** -text
datasets/** -text
research/** -text
schemas/** -text

# Why `datasets/**`: `content_sha256` itself is sha256(`before` + NUL + `after`)
# over the parsed fields (scripts/build-ds2.ts:304, scripts/verify-dataset.ts:780),
# so it does not move with line endings; what does move is `build-ds2.ts --check`,
# which re-emits the tree and compares it byte for byte.
#
# Why `research/**`: `.github/secret_scanning.yml` exempts it from the provider
# shapes, and PROVENANCE.md answers with `sha256:` anchors the gate recomputes -
# a CRLF checkout would break those anchors, not just the exit code.
#
# Why `schemas/**`: `npm run check:schema` re-emits them and fails on any byte
# difference, so a CRLF checkout would read as a schema drift.
